cff-version: 1.2.0
title: The Conceptual Space of Software Architecture
message: >-
  If you use this work, please cite the arXiv version, which is the citable snapshot. Cite the
  specific version that contains the claim you are relying on, since the paper is revised as
  evidence accumulates. Contributors are listed in the acknowledgments of the version they
  contributed to, and in the changelog entry for that version.
abstract: >-
  What is software architecture, such that a machine could hold it? This paper asks whether
  architecture has a small generating basis, a short list of latent variables such that every
  architectural decision is a move along one of them. It answers three ways. Theoretically, it
  derives that an architecture is not a structure but a set of decisions laid over four graphs:
  the problem's constraints, the code's dependencies, the runtime's interactions, and the
  organization's communication. Sixty years of theory keeps rediscovering the same variables
  under different names, and that convergent evidence is treated as the signature of real
  dimensions. Empirically, across eighteen major systems and five industrial migrations, it asks
  of every difference whether it is a choice or decay. Computationally, roughly three quarters of
  the variables are estimable from a bare repository or its git history. The basis is assembled
  into a conceptual space in Gardenfors' sense, with styles as prototype regions, operators and
  tactics as moves, quality attributes as a separate value field, and an asymmetric metric built
  on reachability. It closes with falsifiable predictions.
